You're most likely missing the `BindingContext`. The XAML bindings won't resolve unless the page knows which object to bind against.

**Common fix — set `BindingContext` in code-behind:**

```csharp
public partial class MyPage : ContentPage
{
    public MyPage(MyViewModel vm)
    {
        InitializeComponent();
        BindingContext = vm;
    }
}
```

**Also ensure your ViewModel implements `INotifyPropertyChanged`** (or inherits `ObservableObject` from the MVVM Community Toolkit) so the UI updates when properties change:

```csharp
public partial class MyViewModel : ObservableObject
{
    [ObservableProperty]
    private string title;

    [ObservableProperty]
    private string description;
}
```

**Checklist if it's still blank:**
1. `BindingContext` is set **after** `InitializeComponent()`.
2. Property names match the `{Binding …}` paths exactly (case-sensitive).
3. Properties raise `PropertyChanged` — plain fields/auto-props won't trigger UI updates.
